DATABASES: ERDs & RELATIONSHIPS

1. ENTITY

An Entity represents a database table.

Examples:
- Users
- Customers
- Products
- Students
- Courses

Example:

Users
Customers
Products
Order Details
Payments

Attributes describe entities and represent columns of a table.

Example:

Student
--------
student_id
name
email
age


2. PARENT AND CHILD ENTITIES

Users (Parent)
│
├── Customer (Child)
├── Seller (Child)
└── Admin (Child)


3. WHY RELATIONSHIPS ARE USED

A Database Management System (DBMS) aims to reduce redundancy by creating relationships between tables.

Relational databases describe how one entity is associated with another in a database.


4. PRIMARY KEY

A Primary Key is a column in an entity/table used to uniquely identify a record.

Examples:

Student
-----------------------------------
student_id | name      | age
1          | jack@gmail.com | 21
2          | joy@gmail.com  | 22

Primary Key = student_id


5. EXAMPLE ENTITIES

Course
--------
course_id
course_name

Department
-----------
department_id
department_name

Teacher
--------
teacher_id
department_id
teacher_name
teacher_experience

Class
------
class_id
teacher_id
class_name
class_count

Student
--------
student_id
class_id
name
age


6. TYPES OF RELATIONSHIPS

1. ONE-TO-ONE (1:1)

One record in Entity A is associated with one record in Entity B and vice versa.

Example:

Student ↔ Admission Record

One student has one admission record.
Each admission record belongs to exactly one student.

Diagram:

Student ||------|| Admission


2. ONE-TO-MANY (1:M)

One record in Entity A can be associated with many records in Entity B, but each record in Entity B belongs to only one record in Entity A.

Example:

Class → Students

One class has many students.
Each student belongs to one class.

Diagram:

Class    ||------<
Student


3. MANY-TO-MANY (M:M)

Many records in Entity A can relate to many records in Entity B.

Example:

Students ↔ Subjects

A student studies many subjects.
Each subject is studied by many students.

Diagram:

Student >------< Subject

Implementation:

Student
--------
student_id

Subject
--------
subject_id

Student_Subject
---------------
student_id
subject_id


MEMORY AIDS

One-to-One (1:1)
Person ↔ Passport

One-to-Many (1:M)
Class ↔ Students

Many-to-Many (M:M)
Students ↔ Subjects

    *SQL QUERIES*
->These are SQL statements meant to either insert, retrieve, update or remove data from a database

    *ADDING & FETCHING DATA IN SQL*
For you to add records to a table we use the keyword 'INSERT'

-> Adding new records (rows) to a table
serial->Postgres generates it automatically-> don't need it to add when adding new record

     *Syntax of inserts*
syntax-> how you write it

*INSERT INTO tablename*-> tels postgres which table to add to
    e.g INSERT INTO students
*column names*-> specifies the columns receiving data
    (student_name, age, gender, email)
*values*
-> correspomding values matching your column names

Full syntax: *INSERT INTO table_name(column1, column2, column3...)VALUES(value1, value2, value3,...);*
            
            *INSERT INTO table_name( column1, column2, column3,....)VALUES
                         (value1, value2, value3,...),
                         (value1, value2, value3,...);

->We retrieve data using the key word select
*select*-> retrieve or read data from a table

*Select from every column*
Syntax :   SELECT * FROM table_name;
       eg. SELECT * FROM students;  
       * ->retrieve from all columns 

*Select from specific column*
   ->SELECT column1,column2 FROM table_name;
    e.g, SELECT student_namw, age FROM students;

*Select by filtering*
-> Use the WHERE clause
-> where clause : filter results -> only rows matching a specified condition are returned
  

  between and ordyby


     *Like and not like*
  wildcards
->placeholders for characteres
-> % =>represent single, none or multiple characters 
-> -or_ =>represents single character

eg  SELECT * FROM customers WHERE customername LIKE 'A%'; -> returning customernames that starts with letter A
    SELECT * FROM customers WHERE customername LIKE '%A%'; -> returning customernames with A anywhere

-> Display orders made in the month of January 1997

SELECT * from orders WHERE orderdate LIKE '1997-01%';

 -> Display products without character U in the productname

 SELECT * from products WHERE productname NOT LIKE '%u%';

       *Between and Not between*
->Used to filter records of certain range
 Displayproducts with price range of 10 to 50
 SELECT * from products WHERE 

     *order by*
-> Used to order records in asc or desc using a certain column

=>display products for category 5 from the lowest price
 SELECT * from products WHERE categoryID=5 ORDER BY price;

 =>Display all orderdetails from the highest quantity
 SELECT * from orderdetails ORDER BY quantity DESC;

=>Display all orderdetails for product 11 from the highest quantity
SELECT * from orderdetails WHERE productid=11 ORDER BY quantity DESC;

      Count and group by
-> Used to get the number of records or rows in a table
=> Returns on values
    
      syntax
select count(primary_key/*) from table_name;

select count(productID) where categoryid=1;

1. Count all orders that belong to employee number 5
SELECT count(orderID) from  orders where employeeid= 5;

2. count all orders that belong to shipper number 3
SELECT count(orderID) from  orders where shipperid= 3;

3. count all products from supplier number 4
SELECT count(productid) from  products where supplierid= 4;

      group by
=> Used to group related records

-> Display the number of orders for each customer
select customerid, count(orderid) as number_of_orders from orders group by customerid;

-> Display the number of products for each supplier
SELECT supplierid, count(productid) as number_of_products group by supplierid; 
